From 7620584d63dde253dd5d042d35371dcb26f3d051 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lubo's Ruler <ayazkussann@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 13:31:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 21/21] lubot kapi: ulasilmayan pub fn ratchet'i (39 kapi,
 canary'li)

Twelve crates of pure functions had no answer to one question: does anything call
this? `readme-is-measured` counts tests, `no-panic-path` counts panics, and neither
distinguishes a function wired into a route from one nothing in the tree names - so
the count of unreachable public surface drifted, and each review rediscovered it.

The gate measures: production `pub fn` declarations in `crates/**/src/**/*.rs`
(test tails cut first), against an identifier count over every `.rs`, `.md`,
`.toml`, `.py`, `.json`, `.jsonl` file in the tree, minus the declaring lines in the
declaring file. A name whose only other use sits under `#[cfg(test)]` counts as
unreached on purpose - "tested" and "has a caller" are different claims, and
collapsing them is how a crate of dead API stays green. `/// Convenience:` /
`WIRING:` / `exposed for` within 14 lines above exempts, and the exemption is
visible in the diff rather than in a config.

Ratchet, not ban: `gates/dead-pub-api.baseline` records the 53 entries measured on
this tree (the same shape budlum's `.github/dead-pub-api-baseline.txt` uses), and a
run fails on growth *and* on a stale line, so tightening is the only way to move.
A missing or unsorted baseline fails too: a gate with an unreadable baseline is a
gate that always passes.

Measured here, not asserted: `python3 gates/check.py public-api-is-reached
--self-test` OK; before the baseline existed the gate failed naming the missing
file; after it, OK (53 unreached, baseline 53); `--list` counts 39 gates, and
README plus `training/ratchet.json` moved with it so the ratchet line describes the
tree rather than the previous round. Rust code is untouched, so nothing here needs
a compiler - which matters, because this sandbox has none: `cargo test --workspace`
and `cargo clippy` remain unmeasured for the whole series.
